  "full_response": "Design patterns are reusable solutions to common problems encountered in software design. They represent best practices used by experienced software developers and are templates for how to solve problems in various scenarios. Design patterns can speed up the development process by providing tested, proven development paradigms.\n\n### Observer Pattern\nThe Observer pattern is a behavioral design pattern that allows an object (known as the subject) to notify a list of dependents (observers) about any state changes, usually by calling one of their methods. It's a one-to-many dependency relationship between the subject and its observers; if the subject's state changes, all of its observers are notified.\n\n**Use Cases:**\n- Implementing distributed event-handling systems.\n- Building a model-view-controller (MVC) architecture.\n- Implementing asynchronous data updates in user interfaces.\n\n**Key Components:**\n- **Subject:** Maintains a list of observers and notifies them of any state changes.\n- **Observer:** Defines an updating interface for objects that need to be notified.\n- **ConcreteSubject:** Stores the state of interest to ConcreteObserver objects.\n- **ConcreteObserver:** Maintains a reference to a ConcreteSubject and stores state that should stay consistent with the subject.\n\n### Factory Pattern\nThe Factory pattern is a creational design pattern that provides an interface for creating objects in a superclass but allows subclasses to alter the type of objects that will be created. The Factory pattern is used to abstract the instantiation process.\n\n**Use Cases:**\n- When a class cannot anticipate the type of objects it needs to create.\n- When a class wants its subclasses to specify the objects it creates.\n- When you want to localize the logic required to decide which concrete classes to instantiate.\n\n**Key Components:**\n- **Creator:** Declares the factory method, which returns an object of a Product class. Creator may also define a default implementation of the factory method.\n- **ConcreteCreator:** Overrides the factory method to return an instance of a ConcreteProduct.\n\n### Singleton Pattern\nThe Singleton pattern is a creational design pattern that ensures a class has only one instance while providing a global point of access to it. This is particularly useful when exactly one object is needed to coordinate actions across the system.\n\n**Use Cases:**\n- Managing a connection to a database.\n- Configuring application settings.\n- Logging, where a single log file is shared across the system.\n\n**Key Components:**\n- **Singleton Class:** Ensures only one instance of the class is created and provides a global point of access to that instance.\n\n**Implementation:**\n- Typically",
